svngit (0.1.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * `git apply` now applies renames. A `rename from`/`rename to` patch moves
    the file with `svn move`, so its history survives the commit; a plain
    filesystem move would have committed as an unrelated delete and add.
    Copies are refused rather than mistaken for renames.

 -- Sainan Chelvarajah <sainanc@evertz.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0400

svngit (0.1.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * `git apply` no longer strips one path component too many. The `a/`/`b/`
    prefix was removed while parsing and then counted again by `-p<n>`, so
    any patch touching a file in a subdirectory resolved to the wrong path.

 -- Sainan Chelvarajah <sainanc@evertz.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0400

svngit (0.1.1-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * SECURITY: `git apply` no longer writes outside the working copy. Paths
    were taken from the patch and joined to the working copy root without
    being checked, so a patch containing `..` or an absolute path could
    write any file the user could write. git refuses these; svngit now does
    too.

 -- Sainan Chelvarajah <sainanc@evertz.com>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0400

svngit (0.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release.

 -- Sainan Chelvarajah <sainanc@evertz.com>  Fri, 11 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0400
